{
 "id": "Determinants-Triangular",
 "title_en": "The determinant of a triangular matrix",
 "blocks": [
  {
   "k": "outcome",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "list",
     "ordered": true,
     "items": [
      [
       {
        "k": "p",
        "t": {
         "en": "Calculate the determinant of an upper or lower triangular matrix.",
         "sha": "c318fba8decd",
         "zh": ""
        }
       }
      ]
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "p",
   "t": {
    "en": "There is a certain type of matrix for which finding the determinant is a very simple procedure: a triangular matrix.",
    "sha": "30091dfe61f0",
    "zh": ""
   }
  },
  {
   "k": "box",
   "env": "definition",
   "title": {
    "en": "Triangular matrices",
    "sha": "734bb3a9df74",
    "zh": ""
   },
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "An square matrix $A$ is **upper triangular** if $a_{ij}=0$ whenever $i>j$. In other words, a matrix is upper triangular if the entries below the main diagonal are $0$. Thus, an upper triangular matrix looks as follows, where $\\ast$ refers to any non-zero number:",
      "sha": "cda74005b142",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccccc}\n      \\ast & \\ast & \\cdots & \\ast & \\ast \\\\\n      0 & \\ast & \\cdots & \\ast & \\ast \\\\\n      \\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots & \\vdots \\\\\n      0 & 0 & \\cdots & \\ast & \\ast \\\\\n      0 & 0 & \\cdots & 0 & \\ast \\\\\n    \\end{array}\\right]."
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Similarly, a square matrix is **lower triangular** if all entries above the main diagonal are $0$.",
      "sha": "70333469e458",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "p",
   "t": {
    "en": "The following theorem provides a useful way to calculate the determinant of a triangular matrix.",
    "sha": "2255464aed0c",
    "zh": ""
   }
  },
  {
   "k": "box",
   "env": "theorem",
   "title": {
    "en": "Determinant of a triangular matrix",
    "sha": "465a9344e7dc",
    "zh": ""
   },
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Let $A$ be an upper or lower triangular matrix. Then $\\det(A)$ is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Written as a formula, we have",
      "sha": "5d04c4ce6a12",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "\\det(A) = a_{11}\\,a_{22}\\,\\cdots\\,a_{nn}."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "box",
   "env": "example",
   "title": {
    "en": "Determinant of a triangular matrix",
    "sha": "465a9344e7dc",
    "zh": ""
   },
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Compute $\\det(A)$, where",
      "sha": "24af0802fe83",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "A=\\left[\\begin{array}{rrrr}\n      1 & 2 & 3 & 16 \\\\\n      0 & 2 & 6 & -7 \\\\\n      0 & 0 & 3 & 33 \\\\\n      0 & 0 & 0 & -1\n    \\end{array}\\right]."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "solution",
   "title": null,
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "By Theorem “Determinant of a triangular matrix”, it suffices to take the product of the elements on the main diagonal. Thus",
      "sha": "cd3a81ec2b7f",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "\\det (A) ~=~ 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1) ~=~ -6."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "p",
   "t": {
    "en": "For comparison, let us compute the determinant without Theorem “Determinant of a triangular matrix”, i.e., by using cofactor expansion. If we expand the determinant along the first column, we get:",
    "sha": "d030d897750d",
    "zh": ""
   }
  },
  {
   "k": "math",
   "tex": "\\det (A)\n  ~=~\n  1 \\left|\\begin{array}{rrr}\n      2 & 6 & -7 \\\\\n      0 & 3 & 33 \\\\\n      0 & 0 & -1\n    \\end{array}\\right|\n  - 0 \\left|\\begin{array}{rrr}\n      2 & 3 & 16 \\\\\n      0 & 3 & 33 \\\\\n      0 & 0 & -1\n    \\end{array}\\right|\n  + 0 \\left|\\begin{array}{rrr}\n      2 & 3 & 16 \\\\\n      2 & 6 & -7 \\\\\n      0 & 0 & -1\n    \\end{array}\\right|\n  - 0 \\left|\\begin{array}{rrr}\n      2 & 3 & 16 \\\\\n      2 & 6 & -7 \\\\\n      0 & 3 & 33 \\\\\n    \\end{array}\\right|."
  },
  {
   "k": "p",
   "t": {
    "en": "The only non-zero term in the expansion is",
    "sha": "1254bd0aac71",
    "zh": ""
   }
  },
  {
   "k": "math",
   "tex": "1\\left|\\begin{array}{rrr}\n    2 & 6 & -7 \\\\\n    0 & 3 & 33 \\\\\n    0 & 0 & -1\n  \\end{array}\\right|."
  },
  {
   "k": "p",
   "t": {
    "en": "We can in turns expand this $3\\times 3$ determinant by the cofactor method along the first column:",
    "sha": "eb3194479384",
    "zh": ""
   }
  },
  {
   "k": "math",
   "tex": "\\left|\\begin{array}{rrr}\n    2 & 6 & -7 \\\\\n    0 & 3 & 33 \\\\\n    0 & 0 & -1\n  \\end{array}\\right|\n  ~=~ 2 \\left|\\begin{array}{rr}\n    3 & 33 \\\\\n    0 & -1\n  \\end{array}\\right|\n  - 0 \\left|\\begin{array}{rr}\n    6 & -7 \\\\\n    0 & -1\n  \\end{array}\\right|\n  + 0 \\left|\\begin{array}{rr}\n    6 & -7 \\\\\n    3 & 33 \\\\\n  \\end{array}\\right|."
  },
  {
   "k": "p",
   "t": {
    "en": "Again, the only non-zero term is the first term. In summary,",
    "sha": "72e967e792fe",
    "zh": ""
   }
  },
  {
   "k": "math",
   "tex": "\\det (A)\n  ~=~\n  \\left|\\begin{array}{rrrr}\n    1 & 2 & 3 & 16 \\\\\n    0 & 2 & 6 & -7 \\\\\n    0 & 0 & 3 & 33 \\\\\n    0 & 0 & 0 & -1\n  \\end{array}\\right|\n  ~=~ 1 \\left|\\begin{array}{rrr}\n    2 & 6 & -7 \\\\\n    0 & 3 & 33 \\\\\n    0 & 0 & -1\n  \\end{array}\\right|\n  ~=~ 1\\cdot 2\n  \\left|\\begin{array}{rr}\n    3 & 33 \\\\\n    0 & -1\n  \\end{array}\\right|\n  ~=~ 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)\n  ~=~ -6."
  },
  {
   "k": "p",
   "t": {
    "en": "Of course this is just the same as the product of the diagonal entries of $A$, which is the point of Theorem “Determinant of a triangular matrix”.",
    "sha": "a270e19b81dc",
    "zh": ""
   }
  }
 ],
 "exercises": [
  {
   "id": "ex1",
   "stem": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Find the determinant of the following matrices.",
      "sha": "5df58775f088",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "list",
     "ordered": true,
     "items": [
      [
       {
        "k": "p",
        "t": {
         "en": "$A = \\left[\\begin{array}{rr} 1 & -34 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{array}\\right]$",
         "sha": "cfe2e59f5412",
         "zh": ""
        }
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "k": "p",
        "t": {
         "en": "$B = \\left[\\begin{array}{rrr} 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 1 & 5 \\end{array}\\right]$",
         "sha": "56a3e4a75215",
         "zh": ""
        }
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "k": "p",
        "t": {
         "en": "$C = \\left[\\begin{array}{rrrr} 2 & 3 & 15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{array}\\right]$",
         "sha": "b4841eaabfda",
         "zh": ""
        }
       }
      ]
     ]
    }
   ],
   "sol": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "(a) $\\det(A) = 2$. (b) $\\det(B) = -40$. (c) $\\det(C) = -24$.",
      "sha": "fb0a55686ce5",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ],
